WEBVTT

1-1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:07.347
StackOps is your OS-agnostic digital life manager. It covers 99% of your digital footprint.

1-2
00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:09.055
The applications you use.

1-3
00:00:09.205 --> 00:00:10.570
The way you configure them.

1-4
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.555
Your secrets and credentials.

1-5
00:00:12.705 --> 00:00:14.070
Your files and data.

1-6
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.097
Your code and repositories.

1-7
00:00:16.247 --> 00:00:17.719
And the processes you run.

1-8
00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:19.533
All wrapped into one solution.

2-1
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.385
Start with a fresh, bare machine.

2-2
00:00:22.535 --> 00:00:25.991
Now bring back the environment you've spent ten years making your own.

2-3
00:00:26.141 --> 00:00:31.389
That's the goal: one command, five minutes, and your familiar digital life ready to use.

3-1
00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:35.147
Installing the software is only one part of it.

3-2
00:00:35.297 --> 00:00:41.847
The personal setup matters too: public and private configurations, dotfiles, credentials, and passwords.

3-3
00:00:41.997 --> 00:00:47.522
Then there are your files and data, and the repositories you've mapped out for backup and retrieval.

4-1
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:56.059
StackOps brings package installation, configuration, secrets, and data and code synchronization into one solution.

4-2
00:00:56.209 --> 00:01:03.079
It also launches commands and orchestrates processes, so the same system helps you put that environment to work.

4-3
00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:06.791
Setup, maintenance, and the work you do every day, all connected.

5-1
00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:10.931
Your stack is awesome, but you need a way to manage it all.

5-2
00:01:11.081 --> 00:01:16.031
StackOps doesn't reinvent the wheel. It glues together the best open-source tools.

5-3
00:01:16.181 --> 00:01:21.727
Keep the stack you're comfortable with. StackOps manages the tools and settings that make it yours.

5-4
00:01:21.877 --> 00:01:27.445
It brings that approach across Linux, macOS, and Windows through a command-line interface.

6-1
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.074
That's StackOps. Your digital life, sorted.

6-2
00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:34.531
Bring the setup you've spent years building to the machine in front of you.

6-3
00:01:34.681 --> 00:01:37.113
Explore the documentation to get started.

6-4
00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:41.359
All of it, OS-agnostic. Linux, macOS, and Windows.
